    "reading": {
        "body": "The monthly release count grew from roughly 270 models in early 2025 to 1,815 in August 2026, a more than sixfold increase in under two years, and the pace is still rising.\n\nThe monthly figures show a long plateau through late 2024 and early 2025, followed by a step change upward beginning around January 2026 and then a sharp acceleration from mid-2026 onward. The daily series makes the discontinuity visible: the tracked count sat near 1,185 through mid-August 2026, then jumped to roughly 11,370 around August 22, and jumped again to 16,369 by August 30. Those step changes most likely reflect indexing or classification events rather than a literal tripling of releases in a week, so the daily series should be read as a coverage artifact, not a production signal. The monthly totals, which are less susceptible to that distortion, tell a cleaner story of genuine acceleration.\n\nThe 16,369 models in the catalog span at least six distinct sections, including robotics, scientific, coding, translation, music, and video. Robotics accounts for the largest share of the twelve most recent entries, which is notable because robotics inference is typically latency-sensitive and runs at the edge rather than in a datacenter, creating a different demand profile for compute and power than large language model serving does. The data does not include model size, training compute, or energy cost per model, so it cannot support conclusions about whether this release growth is proportional to growth in underlying compute consumption.",
